From: Kodi Android Blu-ray
Subject: [PATCH] HDMV VM: fix the BC (bit compare) instruction to spec semantics

BC executed the next instruction iff (dst & ~src) == 0 - a subset test
that never checks whether the requested bits are SET, only that no bits
outside the mask are. The BD-ROM spec defines BC as a plain bitwise AND:
the condition passes when any tested bit is set.

Real authoring confirms it: UHD benchmark discs enumerate the UHD
capability PSR bits one at a time (BC PSR25,0x01 / 0x02 / 0x04 / 0x20).
That per-bit enumeration is only meaningful under "is this bit set" -
under the subset test a multi-bit capability register (Kodi reports
UHD_CAP / UHD_DISPLAY_CAP / HDR_PREFERENCE as 0xffffffff genuine OR sets)
fails every single-bit test, so capability queries branched the wrong way.

Analysis credit: Hendrik Leppkes (via SamuriHL/CoreELEC).

--- a/src/libbluray/hdmv/hdmv_vm.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/hdmv/hdmv_vm.c
@@ -1134,7 +1134,12 @@
                 BD_DEBUG(DBG_HDMV|DBG_CRIT, "missing operand in BRANCH/JUMP opcode 0x%08x\n", *(uint32_t*)insn);
             }
             switch (insn->cmp_opt) {
-                case INSN_BC: p->pc += !!(dst & ~src); break;
+                /* spec: plain bitwise AND - the condition passes when any
+                 * tested bit is set. The previous (dst & ~src) subset test
+                 * never checked the requested bits at all and broke
+                 * capability-set registers (PSR25/26) for every disc that
+                 * enumerates them one bit at a time. */
+                case INSN_BC: p->pc += !(dst & src); break;
                 case INSN_EQ: p->pc += !(dst == src); break;
                 case INSN_NE: p->pc += !(dst != src); break;
                 case INSN_GE: p->pc += !(dst >= src); break;
